{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've reproduced the bug in B, G, and H, so the cause is in `_calculate_column_widths`. The flexible column gets its widths from fixed widths plus a flex share, and the flex widths come from `ratio_distribute`. A ratio column with no width has a minimum of 1 plus padding, and `ratio_distribute` takes the max of that minimum and the computed share. I'm checking what happens when the flexible width goes negative, since that looks like where the width is going wrong.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the negative flexible width is what pushes the column to zero, since the minimum of 1 plus padding should normally hold. After the table collapses and gets re-measured, a column that ends up with no width drops to zero. I'm also thinking about how `ratio_distribute` should handle a zero or negative total, so it doesn't hit the assertion.\n\nI'm going to check whether ratio.py has an assertion the tests depend on, since I'm thinking about having ratio_distribute handle a zero or negative total instead of asserting. The flexible columns should keep their 1-plus-padding minimum, but the fallback collapse shrinks everything evenly, which drives the flexible columns down to zero.\n\nNow I'm tracing the numbers: the flexible width comes out at -14, so the minimum of 3 wins and Load gets 3, pushing the table to 47 against a max of 30. The collapse step then only reduces the wrapable column, leaving Load at 0 after the last-resort even reduction.\n\nThe last-resort reduction is what's pushing Load to zero, and it can't respect a per-column minimum because ratio_reduce has no minimums parameter. The table really doesn't fit in 30 columns with the two fixed columns at 22 and 18, so I'll have to decide how overflow should be handled rather than just patch the fallback.\n\nI'm weighing whether to keep the flexible columns at their one-character-plus-padding floor even in the last-resort reduction, which would let the table overflow max_width, versus shrinking the fixed columns instead so everything still fits.\n\nI'm going to protect the flexible columns at their minimum, so the last-resort reduction takes width from the fixed columns first and only lets the table overflow if that still isn't enough.\n\nI'm checking how `ratio_reduce` caps each column's reduction at its width minus its minimum, which means the total may fall short of the excess and some overflow would remain. For non-expanding tables with ratio columns, I'm wondering whether the flexible columns should keep their one-plus-padding minimum regardless of the expand setting.\n\nI'm now thinking about the first collapse path, since `_collapse_widths` can also push a flexible column below its minimum, as the Load column dropping from 3 to 0 in case B shows. One option is to pass the minimums into `_collapse_widths` and cap each reduction at the column's width minus its minimum.\n\nI'm considering making the collapse step skip any flexible column already at its minimum width, so it can't be shrunk further. The catch is that the loop picks its target from the wrapable columns, so I'd need to recompute that set against the current widths on each pass to avoid picking from an empty set.\n\nI'm going to pass a minimums argument into `_collapse_widths`, so the loop only considers wrapable columns that are still above their floor. I need to check that the widest-column reduction can't push a flexible column below its minimum even when only the widest columns are being reduced.\n\nI'm settling on a rule for the reduction: each column can shrink only down to its minimum, so I'll cap each slot at the smaller of the excess, the column's difference from the second widest, and its width minus its minimum. That way ratio_reduce can't push any column below its floor.\n\nStill working through the reduction loop. I'm checking whether it can stall when the widest column is already at its minimum, since the rounding could leave it with nothing to take.\n\nNow I'm working out the last-resort reduction: each flexible column can shrink only down to its minimum, so I'll pass per-slot maximums of width minus minimum into the even reduction. Any overflow that remains after that will have to be left as is.\n\nI'm weighing whether fixed columns should also bottom out at zero during the last-resort even reduction, since the flexible columns are the ones I'm protecting. The fixed widths stay as they are today, and only the flexible columns need the minimum floor.\n\nI'm checking the ratio distribution step, where a negative flexible total gets clamped to the column minimum, and I want to confirm that the re-measured widths keep flexible columns at one character plus padding.\n\nI'm considering whether `ratio_distribute` should bail out when the total is zero or negative rather than hitting the assertion. I also want to check whether `tests/test_ratio.py` relies on that assertion. The case I'm worried about is an expanding table where every column measures zero width, which would make the ratio sum zero.\n\nI'm checking where the total could reach zero: after the last-resort re-measure, or with an empty table that has no columns. Ratio columns set to 0 are also in question, since the `any(ratios)` guard might skip them in a way that matters.\n\nStill checking the division in `ratio_distribute`, where the total ratio could come out to zero and cause a division error.\n\nI'm checking whether `ratio_distribute` can hit a zero or negative total when no ratio is positive, and I'm considering having it fall back to distributing by the minimums in that case instead of tripping the assertion.\n\nI'm making `ratio_distribute` handle a zero or negative total by falling back to an even split across the slots, so it won't hit the assertion.\n\nI'm settling on clamping the total to zero so a negative value can't produce negative widths, and I'm checking whether the minimums still hold when the total is clamped.\n\nI'm going to clamp the total so a negative value can't produce negative widths, and then check the existing tests for `ratio_distribute` to see whether they depend on the assertion.\n\n","thinkingSignature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1oYWlrdS01LjUtMjAyNjEwMDd8Y2xhdWRlLW9uLWF3cyJ9"},{"type":"text","text":"Reproduced: cases B, G and H show the `||` collapse. Root cause: `flexible_width` goes negative, the flex column is set to its minimum, then the overflow path (`_collapse_widths` and the last-resort `ratio_reduce`) shrinks that column below its minimum, down to 0. Let me check what the tests expect from `ratio_distribute` before changing it."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_015VQUHG22Crk8cPNNAe81nt","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /app && grep -n \"raises\\|Sum of\\|ratio_distribute\\|_collapse_widths\" -r tests | head -30; cat tests/test_ratio.py | head -80"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"openrouter","model":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5999,"cacheRead":10756,"cacheWrite":1897,"totalTokens":18654,"cost":{"input":2.0000000000000002e-7,"output":0.0029995,"cacheRead":0.00010756000000000001,"cacheWrite":0.000237125,"total":0.003344385,"totalFromProvider":true},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":5766},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794165850432,"providerThinkingLevel":"medium","responseId":"msg_011Cgr1Pva2YtUrTck2CHJdK","rawStopReason":"tool_use","durationMs":29730,"thinkingLevel":"medium"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_015VQUHG22Crk8cPNNAe81nt","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"tests/.pytest_cache/v/cache/nodeids:512:  \"test_pretty.py::test_ipy_display_hook__special_repr_raises_exception\",\ntests/.pytest_cache/v/cache/nodeids:908:  \"test_tools.py::test_ratio_distribute\",\ntests/test_align.py:17:    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\ntests/test_align.py:19:    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\ntests/test_align.py:21:    with pytest.raises(ValueError